 "meta_notes": {
  "headline": "doc 53 SS4.1's comparator, registered as a predicted TIE with R8 and never implemented in the main run, is run here. M2: comparator 1.0000 vs R8 0.7387 (delta +0.2613). M3: comparator 0.8400 vs R8 0.6973 (delta +0.1427). Verdict comparator_matches_or_exceeds_R8 = True.",
  "arity_converts_classically": "The comparator's OWN arity sweep is the sharp descriptive finding. M2: M=2 0.7600 -> M=4 1.0000 -> M=8 1.0000. M3: M=2 0.6640 -> M=4 0.8360 -> M=8 0.8400. Classically the Davenport-Schinzel budget DOES convert into trained accuracy; in exp67's circuit it did not (arity_lifts = FALSE, and R4 > R8 at both cells). That contrast locates exp67's negative on the OPTIMISATION side of the circuit, not in the decision-complexity budget itself. Recorded as descriptive: no exp67 criterion was registered against it.",
  "instrument_corroboration": "The comparator lands on doc 53 SS2's INDEPENDENTLY computed ceiling table without being fitted to it: M2 at M=4 and M=8 reaches 1.0000 against ceilings 1.0000/1.0000, and M3 at M=4 reaches 0.8360 against a ceiling of 0.8400. Both binary fits sit BELOW their binary ceilings (M2 0.7600 vs 0.8750; M3 0.6640 vs 0.7778), as they must. This is a consistency check on the feature frame and the ceiling instrument, not a criterion. M3 at M=8 reaches 0.8400 against a ceiling of 1.0000 -- the held-out inventory at m=3 carries a single noun in one band (doc 47 SS5 registered limitation), so 0.84 is where held-out separability, not decision complexity, binds.",
  "where_the_comparator_loses": "The single place the comparator does NOT beat the circuit is the binary cell: M2 comparator M=2 0.7600 vs R2 0.7913 (-0.0313), M3 0.6640 vs 0.6613 (+0.0027). The function-class note explains it -- the circuit carries twelve 9-dim forms (one per verb x event-direction) where the registered comparator carries one. It is not part of the SS4.1 criterion, which is comparator vs R8.",
  "what_it_does_not_change": "This comparator NEVER touched arity_lifts, which is R8 - R2 inside exp67 and remains FALSE. SS4.1's only job is to block a quantum reading of a POSITIVE R8 -- and R8 was not positive, so the guard was never load-bearing. Running it late closes a registration debt; it does not move a verdict.",
  "run_once_per_cell_interpretation": "'Run once per cell' is read as ONE deterministic no-restart fit per (cell, M, angle draw), at the FIXED registered seed 67001 -- not one fit total. exp67's per-item inputs are seed-indexed (band jitter is drawn at random.Random(62500 + seed)), so its R8 mean is a mean over five angle geometries; the comparator is fitted on those SAME five and mean is compared to mean. Per-draw deltas are recorded so a single-draw reading can be recovered from this file.",
  "descriptive_not_promoted": "M = 8 is the registered comparison. M = 4 and M = 2 are recorded as DESCRIPTIVE ONLY and are never promoted onto the SS4.1 criterion, per the exp44 lesson already invoked in results_exp67.json.",
  "function_class": "The 9-dim basis closes the circuit's outcome probabilities to 2.1e-07 only when conditioned on BOTH the verb and the event direction (12 groups); pooled it is 0.66, by direction alone 0.44, by verb alone 0.61. So the circuit carries TWELVE 9-dim forms and the registered comparator carries ONE. The label is verb-independent and the twin swap is absorbed by the (agent, patient) ordering, so the Bayes-relevant structure is expressible in the single form -- but the comparator is strictly a RESTRICTION of the circuit's conditional function class, which is recorded here rather than glossed. It is also the natural explanation for the one place the comparator UNDERperforms: the binary M = 2 comparator vs R2.",
  "scope": "Same dataset (sha ae94ab9c), same stratified exp62_bands assignment, same train/val/test splits, same fixed alternating grouping and same logsumexp(4.0)/scale-3.0 surrogate as exp67. No new data, no new dataset draw, no exp67 artefact modified."
 },
